      "text": "A nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients can have irrational roots at most in conjugate pairs. Therefore, only the numbers $1+\\sqrt{2}$ and $2+\\sqrt{3}$ are fully necessary to construct a polynomial with rational coefficients since they are the smallest numbers in the list of roots (just to satisfy the problem's statement). There are therefore 1000 roots, which can be written in this general formula: $\\boxed{(x-1-\\sqrt{2})(x-2-\\sqrt{3})...(x-1000-\\sqrt{1001})}$.\n\nfor reference, the quadratic expressions  $\\left(x-1-\\sqrt{2}\\right)\\left(x-1+\\sqrt{2}\\right) = x^{2}-2x-1$ and  $\\left(x-2-\\sqrt{3}\\right)\\left(x-2+\\sqrt{3}\\right) = x^{2}-4x-1$  are used.",
