    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that the polynomial has rational coefficients and contains the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\;2+\\sqrt{3}, \\;3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\;1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, it must also include their conjugates to ensure that the polynomial has rational coefficients. The conjugates are \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\;2-\\sqrt{3}, \\;3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\;1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nTherefore, the polynomial must include all numbers from \\(1+\\sqrt{2}\\) to \\(1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) and all their conjugates \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\;2-\\sqrt{3}, \\;3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\;1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nThe numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\;2+\\sqrt{3}, \\;3+\\sqrt{4},\\; \\dots, \\;1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) and their conjugates \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\;2-\\sqrt{3}, \\;3-\\sqrt{4},\\; \\dots, \\;1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\) make up the polynomial. Since we are including both each number and its conjugate, each number contributes 2 terms to the polynomial (one positive and one negative root).\n\nThus, the total number of distinct roots is given by:\n\\[\n(1000+\\sqrt{1001}) - (1-\\sqrt{2}) + c,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) accounts for the total count of distinct roots, assuming repetition.\n\nSimplifying the expression:\n\\[\n1000 + \\sqrt{1001} - 1 + \\sqrt{2} = 999 + \\sqrt{1001} + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis series contains several pairs of conjugate roots, adding up:\nEach pair of conjugate roots contributes exactly 2 terms to the polynomial, and 1000 distinct numbers each give 2 terms per root.\n\nTherefore, the total number of distinct roots in the polynomial is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 1000 = 2000.\n\\]\nThus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2000}.\n\\]",
